tory", "url": "https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-decider"}, {"by": "juscha1", "descendants": 71, "id": 50487228, "kids": [50490242, 50488197, 50487747, 50489341, 50489116, 50487812, 50487783, 50490413, 50489743, 50487765, 50490489, 50488981, 50490455, 50489691, 50487887, 50489230, 50488784, 50488534, 50490456, 50493575, 50489051, 50492480, 50487714, 50493779, 50489708, 50492511, 50489153, 50487635, 50487941, 50491861, 50492394, 50490486, 50491325, 50490874, 50490773], "score": 219, "text": "Technical Write-up: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.justinchavez.com&#x2F;blog&#x2F;isometric-sf-how&#x2F;\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.justinchavez.com&#x2F;blog&#x2F;isometric-sf-how&#x2F;</a><p>Hi HN! Showcasing this fun project. I took 1300 square miles of the SF Bay Area from Google Photorealistic 3D Tiles and transformed them into a cohesive pixel map. It&#x27;s really fun to explore and stumble upon various famous landmarks like Apple Park, Palace of Fine Arts, or UC Berkeley.<p>The technicals involve using Nano Banana 2 to train a Qwen-Image-Edit 2511 LoRA which is used to prototype the style and generation methods. For final map I used Qwen to train a Klein 4B LoRA which reproduced the style at 1&#x2F;4 of the cost. More details in the blog post.<p>Let me know what you think! I really enjoyed this process of fine-tuning smaller open source models.<p>If I did the map with Nano Banana 2 it would have cost around 7k USD!<p>Credit for the idea goes to Isometric NYC.", "time": 1794592717, "title": "Show HN: Isometric SF – A pixel-art map of the SF Bay Area", "type": "story", "url": "https://isometricsf.com/"}, {"by": "JohnDohnBohn", "descendants": 28, "id": 50488835, "kids": [50490992, 50488836, 50491053, 50491679, 50493564, 50491584, 50490306, 50493025, 50489686], "score": 114, "time": 1794599922, "title": "Live-migration support in FreeBSD and bhyve coming", "type": "story", "url": "https://gyptazy.com/blog/freebsd-bhyve-live-migration-support-rfc/"}